# GPU Execution Plane — Architectural Invariants

These rules are non-negotiable. Violating any of them breaks the system contract with SynAnton Core.

## 1. Domain isolation

**Domain MUST NOT depend on:** Kubernetes, vLLM, protobuf classes, PostgreSQL/JDBC, Spring adapters,
infrastructure clients, or any class outside the `com.synanton.gpu.domain` package tree.

Domain interfaces (`ExecutionRepository`, `ExecutionRuntime`, `ArtifactResolver`, `ModelRepository`,
`ExecutionScheduler`) are the only boundary the domain may cross.

## 2. PostgreSQL is the authoritative execution-state store

Execution state (current phase, timestamps, usage, error) lives exclusively in the `executions`
PostgreSQL table. No in-memory cache, no Redis, no Cassandra may replicate or cache execution state.

## 3. Redis MUST NOT be introduced

Redis must not be added for execution state, idempotency, admission counters, or any other
GPU-plane concern. PostgreSQL advisory locks handle admission serialization.

## 4. Topology leakage is forbidden

SynAnton Core (the primary platform) MUST NOT receive Kubernetes pod names, node IPs, GPU device
IDs, vLLM internal endpoints, or any runtime topology detail. The `synanton.gpu.v1` gRPC contract
is the only surface visible to Core.

## 5. Runtime retries MUST respect RetryDisposition

Runtime failures are classified as one of:
- `NOT_ACCEPTED` — safe to retry (request never accepted by runtime)
- `ACCEPTED_UNKNOWN` — may have executed; caller must check status
- `COMPLETED_UNKNOWN` — may be complete; caller must check status
- `DEFINITELY_FAILED` — do not retry

Classification is based on HTTP status codes and response headers, **not** caught Java exceptions.
Generic `IOException` / `RuntimeException` retry loops are forbidden.

## 6. CPU fallback MUST NOT exist in this repository

CPU fallback policy belongs to SynAnton Core (`../platform`). If the GPU plane cannot execute,
it returns an error. Core decides whether to fall back to CPU-based inference.

## 7. Equalix is optional and must implement ExecutionScheduler

`EqualixScheduler` (if ever built) MUST implement `ExecutionScheduler` and be wired via Spring
configuration only. `ExecuteService` and all domain code MUST NOT reference Equalix directly.
`DirectScheduler` remains the default.

## 8. Sensitive data MUST NOT be logged

Never log: prompts, token contents, embedding vectors, authorization assertions, or API keys.
Log only: execution_id, request_id, tenant_id, model_id, state transitions, error codes.

## 9. Execution state transitions MUST be validated by the domain

`ExecutionState.canTransitionTo(next)` guards every state change. Adapters (JDBC, gRPC) must call
this guard before persisting a new state. It is impossible to reach an invalid state through
normal code paths.

## 10. All concurrency admission decisions MUST be transactionally safe

The admission check (COUNT active executions per model) and the admission INSERT must execute
inside a single PostgreSQL transaction with a model-level advisory lock:

```sql
SELECT pg_advisory_xact_lock(?)   -- model lock key
SELECT COUNT(*) FROM executions WHERE model_id = ? AND state NOT IN ('SUCCEEDED','FAILED','CANCELLED')
INSERT INTO executions ...
```

Two concurrent Execute calls cannot both consume the same concurrency slot.
